{"task_id": "HumanEval/50", "solution": "```python\ndef encode_shift(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    returns encoded string by shifting every character by 5 in the alphabet.\n    \"\"\"\n    return \"\".join([chr(((ord(ch) + 5 - ord(\"a\")) % 26) + ord(\"a\")) for ch in s])\n\n\ndef decode_shift(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    takes as input string encoded with encode_shift function. Returns decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class Alphabet:\n        \"\"\"Helper class to manage lowercase English alphabet operations.\"\"\"\n        _letters = \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\"\n        _size = 26\n\n        def get_index_for_char(self, char: str) -> int:\n            return ord(char) - ord('a')\n\n        def get_char_at_index(self, index: int) -> str:\n            # Ensures the index wraps within the alphabet size.\n            return chr((index % self._size) + ord('a'))\n\n    alphabet_helper = Alphabet()\n    decoded_chars = []\n\n    for char_code in s:\n        current_index = alphabet_helper.get_index_for_char(char_code)\n        # Shift backward by 5. Add _size before modulo to handle negative intermediate results.\n        shifted_index = (current_index - 5 + alphabet_helper._size) % alphabet_helper._size\n        decoded_char = alphabet_helper.get_char_at_index(shifted_index)\n        decoded_chars.append(decoded_char)\n\n    return \"\".join(decoded_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "```\n```python\nimport math\n\n\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\n\n\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(xs)\n\n    # Validate constraints as per problem description:\n    # \"find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\"\n    # \"and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees a solution.\"\n    # The problem description implies these are preconditions, so a ValueError can be raised if violated.\n    if n % 2 != 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list 'xs' must contain an even number of coefficients.\")\n    if n == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Coefficients list cannot be empty.\")\n    if xs[-1] == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"The largest coefficient (xs[-1]) must be non-zero as it guarantees a solution.\")\n\n    if n == 2:  # Linear polynomial: a0 + a1*x = 0\n        # Given the guarantee, xs[1] (a1) will not be zero.\n        return -xs[0] / xs[1]\n    else:\n        # For higher even lengths (implying odd degree polynomials),\n        # use a bisection method as a general numerical root-finding approach.\n        # This is a robust method when a root is guaranteed within an interval.\n\n        # Establish a sufficiently wide initial search interval.\n        # For odd-degree polynomials, f(x) approaches +/- infinity as x approaches +/- infinity.\n        # This guarantees a sign change over a sufficiently large interval.\n        low = -1_000_000.0\n        high = 1_000_000.0\n        tolerance = 1e-6\n        max_iterations = 100\n\n        f_low = poly(xs, low)\n        f_high = poly(xs, high)\n\n        # Check if the initial interval already brackets a root.\n        # For odd-degree polynomials and large enough bounds, this should always be true.\n        # If by chance f_low and f_high have the same sign (e.g., due to numerical issues\n        # or an extremely narrow or non-root-bracketing initial interval),\n        # a more sophisticated bracket finding or initial guess strategy would be needed.\n        # However, given the problem's guarantees and typical behavior of odd-degree polynomials,\n        # we assume this setup is sufficient.\n        if f_low * f_high > 0:\n            # Attempt to widen the interval if the initial one doesn't bracket a root\n            # (though with -1M to 1M, this is unlikely for odd-degree polynomials).\n            # This is a fallback and might indicate an issue with the initial bounds\n            # or a very unusual polynomial. For strict compliance with \"guarantees a solution\"\n            # and a numerical method, we should ensure a bracket.\n            # For this problem, we'll proceed assuming the large bounds are sufficient.\n            pass # No action needed, as the problem guarantees a solution.\n\n        for _ in range(max_iterations):\n            mid = (low + high) / 2.0\n            f_mid = poly(xs, mid)\n\n            if abs(f_mid) < tolerance:\n                return mid\n\n            # Narrow down the interval based on sign change\n            if f_low * f_mid < 0:\n                high = mid\n                f_high = f_mid # Update f_high for the next iteration\n            else:\n                low = mid\n                f_low = f_mid # Update f_low for the next iteration\n\n        # If max_iterations reached, return the midpoint of the final interval\n        # as the best approximation found.\n        return (low + high) / 2.0\n\n```"}
