{"task_id": "HumanEval/81", "solution": "def numerical_letter_grade(grades):\n    \"\"\"It is the last week of the semester and the teacher has to give the grades\n    to students. The teacher has been making her own algorithm for grading.\n    The only problem is, she has lost the code she used for grading.\n    She has given you a list of GPAs for some students and you have to write\n    a function that can output a list of letter grades using the following table:\n             GPA       |    Letter grade\n              4.0                A+\n            > 3.7                A\n            > 3.3                A-\n            > 3.0                B+\n            > 2.7                B\n            > 2.3                B-\n            > 2.0                C+\n            > 1.7                C\n            > 1.3                C-\n            > 1.0                D+\n            > 0.7                D\n            > 0.0                D-\n              0.0                E\n\n\n    Example:\n    grade_equation([4.0, 3, 1.7, 2, 3.5]) ==> ['A+', 'B', 'C-', 'C', 'A-']\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for gpa in grades:\n        if gpa == 4.0:\n            result.append('A+')\n        elif gpa > 3.7:\n            result.append('A')\n        elif gpa > 3.3:\n            result.append('A-')\n        elif gpa > 3.0:\n            result.append('B+')\n        elif gpa > 2.7:\n            result.append('B')\n        elif gpa > 2.3:\n            result.append('B-')\n        elif gpa > 2.0:\n            result.append('C+')\n        elif gpa > 1.7:\n            result.append('C')\n        elif gpa > 1.3:\n            result.append('C-')\n        elif gpa > 1.0:\n            result.append('D+')\n        elif gpa > 0.7:\n            result.append('D')\n        elif gpa > 0.0:\n            result.append('D-')\n        elif gpa == 0.0:\n            result.append('E')\n        else:\n            result.append('E') # GPAs less than 0.0 or invalid inputs default to 'E' based on grading table for 0.0\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        # For n=1, the only 1-digit number that starts or ends with 1 is '1'.\n        return 1\n    else:\n        # Calculate numbers starting with 1.\n        # The first digit is 1, the remaining (n-1) digits can be any of 0-9.\n        count_starts_with_1 = 10**(n - 1)\n\n        # Calculate numbers ending with 1.\n        # The last digit is 1. The first digit can be 1-9 (9 choices), and the\n        # middle (n-2) digits can be any of 0-9 (10 choices each).\n        count_ends_with_1 = 9 * (10**(n - 2))\n\n        # Calculate numbers that start with 1 AND end with 1.\n        # The first digit is 1, the last digit is 1. The middle (n-2) digits\n        # can be any of 0-9.\n        count_starts_and_ends_with_1 = 10**(n - 2)\n\n        # Apply the inclusion-exclusion principle: |A U B| = |A| + |B| - |A intersection B|\n        total_count = count_starts_with_1 + count_ends_with_1 - count_starts_and_ends_with_1\n        return total_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/86", "solution": "```python\ndef anti_shuffle(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function that takes a string and returns an ordered version of it.\n    Ordered version of string, is a string where all words (separated by space)\n    are replaced by a new word where all the characters arranged in\n    ascending order based on ascii value.\n    Note: You should keep the order of words and blank spaces in the sentence.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = []\n    current_word = []\n\n    # Iterate through the string to identify words and spaces\n    for char in s:\n        if char == ' ':\n            # If a word was being built, sort it and add to parts\n            if current_word:\n                parts.append(\"\".join(sorted(current_word)))\n                current_word = []\n            parts.append(' ') # Add the space\n        else:\n            current_word.append(char)\n\n    # Add the last word if exists\n    if current_word:\n        parts.append(\"\".join(sorted(current_word)))\n\n    return \"\".join(parts)\n```"}
